						<h2><font size=3D"3" color=3D"#000000">
						<span style=3D"font-weight: 400">5. Capturing New Projects=20
						&amp; Tasks</span></font></h2>
						<p><font size=3D"2">I mentioned before that writing things down to ge=
t them out of your=20
						head and into your productivity system is a very good=20
						time management practice.</font></p>
						<p><font size=3D"2">Achieve Planner makes this very quick=20
						and easy. You can always enter projects and tasks=20
						directly into the Outline, but you can also use the=20
						'Quick Task Entry' feature to speed up the process.</font></p>
						<p><font size=3D"2">You can access the Quick Task Entry=20
						from the Achieve Planner notification icon in the=20
						Windows taskbar or by using a configurable Hot Key (Alt+Win+T)=20
						that you can invoke from any application.</font></p>
						<p align=3D"center">
						<img border=3D"0" src=3D"http://www.effexis.com/achieve/screenshots19=
/QuickTaskEntryDialog.png" width=3D"520" height=3D"390"></p>
						<p align=3D"left"><font size=3D"2">From here, you can create=20
						one or more tasks (using indentation to create=20
						subtasks).</font></p>
						<p align=3D"left"><font size=3D"2">By default, all new tasks=20
						created this way are stored under the 'Inbox' project=20
						for later processing, but you can also assign them to a=20
						specific project instead.</font></p>
						<p align=3D"left"><font size=3D"2">Video Tutorial (link=20
						opens in new window):</font></p>
						<ul>
							<li>
							<p align=3D"left"><font size=3D"2">
							<a target=3D"_blank" href=3D"http://www.effexis.com/achieve/tour/GMD=
T-AP-Lesson6-T2-QuickTaskEntry.htm">Quick Task Entry</a> - How to use the q=
uick task entry to capture tasks=20
while you are working on something else</font></p></li>
						</ul>
						<p align=3D"left">6. Organizing New Projects/Tasks</p>
						<p align=3D"left"><font size=3D"2">When you capture new=20
						projects/tasks without specifying a parent project,=20
						Achieve Planner stores them in a special <b>Inbox</b>=20
						project for later processing.</font></p>
						<p align=3D"left"><font size=3D"2">When you are ready to=20
						process them, you can use the <b>New Task Organizer=20
						Wizard</b> (available from the Tools menu) to help you=20
						decide how to handle them...</font></p>
						<p align=3D"center">
						<a target=3D"_blank" href=3D"http://www.effexis.com/achieve/screensho=
ts19/NewTaskOrganizerSS.png">
						<img border=3D"0" src=3D"http://www.effexis.com/achieve/screenshots19=
/NewTaskOrganizerSS-TN.png" width=3D"520" height=3D"451"></a><br>
						<font size=3D"1">Click Image to View Larger Size</font></p>
						<p align=3D"left"><font size=3D"2">The Wizard helps you=20
						decide what type of item it is (a project, a task, a=20
						non-actionable note/reference item, or something to=20
						delete right away.)</font></p>
						<p align=3D"left"><font size=3D"2">You can then change the=20
						name/priority, set the parent, and add other details=20
						like context and deadline.</font></p>
						<p align=3D"left"><font size=3D"2">You can also <b>defer</b>=20
						the item to a future date for later processing (like a=20
						tickler file).</font></p>
						<p align=3D"left"><font size=3D"2">Video Tutorial (link=20
						opens in new window):</font></p>
						<ul>
							<li>
							<p align=3D"left"><font size=3D"2">
							<a target=3D"_blank" href=3D"http://www.effexis.com/achieve/tour/GMD=
